.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt OPENSSL-QUIC 7ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm openssl-quic
.Nd OpenSSL QUIC
.Sh DESCRIPTION
OpenSSL 3.2 and later features support for the QUIC transport protocol.
You can use OpenSSL's QUIC capabilities for both client and server
applications.
This man page describes how to let applications use the QUIC protocol
using the libssl API.
.Pp
The QUIC protocol maps to the standard SSL API. A QUIC connection is
represented by an SSL object in the same way that a TLS connection is.
Only minimal changes are needed to existing applications which use
libssl API to bring QUIC protocol support in.
QUIC clients can use
.Xr OSSL_QUIC_client_method 3
or
.Xr OSSL_QUIC_client_thread_method 3
with
.Xr SSL_CTX_new 3 .
See below for more details about the difference between the two.
For servers, there is only one option: SSL method
.Xr OSSL_QUIC_server_method 3
with
.Xr SSL_CTX_new 3 .
.Pp
The remainder of this man page discusses, in order:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It
Default stream mode versus multi-stream mode for clients;
.It
The changes to existing libssl APIs which are driven by QUIC-related
implementation requirements, which existing applications should bear in
mind;
.It
Aspects which must be considered by existing applications when adopting
QUIC, including potential changes which may be needed.
.It
Recommended usage approaches for new applications.
.It
New, QUIC-specific APIs.
.El
.Sh CLIENT MODES OF OPERATION
When a client creates a QUIC connection, by default, it operates in
default stream mode, which is intended to provide compatibility with
existing non-QUIC application usage patterns.
In this mode, the connection has a single stream associated with it.
Calls to
.Xr SSL_read 3
and
.Xr SSL_write 3
on the QUIC connection SSL object read and write from that stream.
Whether the stream is client-initiated or server-initiated from a QUIC
perspective depends on whether
.Xr SSL_read 3
or
.Xr SSL_write 3
is called first.
.Pp
Default stream mode is primarily for compatibility with existing
applications.
For new applications utilizing QUIC, it's recommended to disable this
mode and instead adopt the multi-stream API. See the RECOMMENDATIONS FOR
NEW APPLICATIONS section for more details.
.Ss Default Stream Mode
A QUIC client connection can be used in either default stream mode or
multi-stream mode.
By default, a newly created QUIC connection SSL object uses default
stream mode.
.Pp
In default stream mode, a stream is implicitly created and bound to the
QUIC connection SSL object;
.Xr SSL_read 3
and
.Xr SSL_write 3
calls to the QUIC connection SSL object work by default and are mapped
to that stream.
.Pp
When default stream mode is used, any API function which can be called
on a QUIC stream SSL object can also be called on a QUIC connection SSL
object, in which case it affects the default stream bound to the
connection.
.Pp
The identity of a QUIC stream, including its stream ID, varies depending
on whether a stream is client-initiated or server-initiated.
In default stream mode, if a client application calls
.Xr SSL_read 3
first before any call to
.Xr SSL_write 3
on the connection, it is assumed that the application protocol is using
a server-initiated stream, and the
.Xr SSL_read 3
call will not complete (either blocking, or failing appropriately if
nonblocking mode is configured) until the server initiates a stream.
Conversely, if the client application calls
.Xr SSL_write 3
before any call to
.Xr SSL_read 3
on the connection, it is assumed that a client-initiated stream is to be
used and such a stream is created automatically.
.Pp
Default stream mode is intended to aid compatibility with legacy
applications.
New applications adopting QUIC should use multi-stream mode, described
below, and avoid use of the default stream functionality.
.Pp
It is possible to use additional streams in default stream mode using
.Xr SSL_new_stream 3
and
.Xr SSL_accept_stream 3 ;
note that the default incoming stream policy will need to be changed
using
.Xr SSL_set_incoming_stream_policy 3
in order to use
.Xr SSL_accept_stream 3
in this case.
However, applications using additional streams are strongly recommended
to use multi-stream mode instead.
.Pp
Calling
.Xr SSL_new_stream 3
or
.Xr SSL_accept_stream 3
before a default stream has been associated with the QUIC connection SSL
object will inhibit future creation of a default stream.
.Ss Multi-Stream Mode
The recommended usage mode for new applications adopting QUIC is
multi-stream mode, in which no default stream is attached to the QUIC
connection SSL object and attempts to call
.Xr SSL_read 3
and
.Xr SSL_write 3
on the QUIC connection SSL object fail.
Instead, an application calls
.Xr SSL_new_stream 3
or
.Xr SSL_accept_stream 3
to create individual stream SSL objects for sending and receiving
application data using
.Xr SSL_read 3
and
.Xr SSL_write 3 .
.Pp
To use multi-stream mode, call
.Xr SSL_set_default_stream_mode 3
with an argument of
.Sy SSL_DEFAULT_STREAM_MODE_NONE ;
this function must be called prior to initiating the connection.
The default stream mode cannot be changed after initiating a connection.
.Pp
When multi-stream mode is used, meaning that no default stream is
associated with the connection, calls to API functions which are defined
as operating on a QUIC stream fail if called on the QUIC connection SSL
object.
For example, calls such as
.Xr SSL_write 3
or
.Xr SSL_get_stream_id 3
will fail.
.Sh CHANGES TO EXISTING APIS
Most SSL APIs, such as
.Xr SSL_read 3
and
.Xr SSL_write 3 ,
function as they do for TLS connections and do not have changed
semantics, with some exceptions.
The changes to the semantics of existing APIs are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It
Since QUIC uses UDP,
.Xr SSL_set_bio 3 ,
.Xr SSL_set0_rbio 3
and
.Xr SSL_set0_wbio 3
function as before, but must now receive a BIO with datagram semantics.
There are broadly four options for applications to use as a network BIO:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It
.Xr BIO_s_datagram 3 ,
recommended for most applications, replaces
.Xr BIO_s_socket 3
and provides a UDP socket.
.It
.Xr BIO_s_dgram_pair 3
provides BIO pair-like functionality but with datagram semantics, and is
recommended for existing applications which use a BIO pair or memory BIO
to manage libssl's communication with the network.
.It
.Xr BIO_s_dgram_mem 3
provides a simple memory BIO-like interface but with datagram semantics.
Unlike
.Xr BIO_s_dgram_pair 3 ,
it is unidirectional.
.It
An application may also choose to implement a custom BIO. The new
.Xr BIO_sendmmsg 3
and
.Xr BIO_recvmmsg 3
APIs must be supported.
.El
.It
.Xr SSL_set_fd 3 ,
.Xr SSL_set_rfd 3
and
.Xr SSL_set_wfd 3
traditionally instantiate a
.Xr BIO_s_socket 3 .
For QUIC, these functions instead instantiate a
.Xr BIO_s_datagram 3 .
This is equivalent to instantiating a
.Xr BIO_s_datagram 3
and using
.Xr SSL_set0_rbio 3
and
.Xr SSL_set0_wbio 3 .
.It
Traditionally, whether the application-level I/O APIs (such as
.Xr SSL_read 3
and
.Xr SSL_write 3
operated in a blocking fashion was directly correlated with whether the
underlying network socket was configured in a blocking fashion.
This is no longer the case; applications must explicitly configure the
desired application-level blocking mode using
.Xr SSL_set_blocking_mode 3 .
See
.Xr SSL_set_blocking_mode 3
for details.
.It
Network-level I/O must always be performed in a nonblocking manner.
The application can still enjoy blocking semantics for calls to
application-level I/O functions such as
.Xr SSL_read 3
and
.Xr SSL_write 3 ,
but the underlying network BIO provided to QUIC (such as a
.Xr BIO_s_datagram 3 )
must be configured in nonblocking mode.
For application-level blocking functionality, see
.Xr SSL_set_blocking_mode 3 .
.It
.Xr BIO_new_ssl_connect 3
has been changed to automatically use a
.Xr BIO_s_datagram 3
when used with QUIC, therefore applications which use this do not need
to change the BIO they use.
.It
.Xr BIO_new_buffer_ssl_connect 3
cannot be used with QUIC and applications must change to use
.Xr BIO_new_ssl_connect 3
instead.
.It
.Xr SSL_shutdown 3
has significant changes in relation to how QUIC connections must be shut
down.
In particular, applications should be advised that the full
RFC-conformant QUIC shutdown process may take an extended amount of
time.
This may not be suitable for short-lived processes which should exit
immediately after their usage of a QUIC connection is completed.
A rapid shutdown mode is available for such applications.
For details, see
.Xr SSL_shutdown 3 .
.It
.Xr SSL_want 3 ,
.Xr SSL_want_read 3
and
.Xr SSL_want_write 3
no longer reflect the I/O state of the network BIO passed to the QUIC
SSL object, but instead reflect the flow control state of the QUIC
stream associated with the SSL object.
.Pp
When used in nonblocking mode,
.Sy SSL_ERROR_WANT_READ
indicates that the receive part of a QUIC stream does not currently have
any more data available to be read, and
.Sy SSL_ERROR_WANT_WRITE
indicates that the stream's internal buffer is full.
.Pp
To determine if the QUIC implementation currently wishes to be informed
of incoming network datagrams, use the new function
.Xr SSL_net_read_desired 3 ;
likewise, to determine if the QUIC implementation currently wishes to be
informed when it is possible to transmit network datagrams, use the new
function
.Xr SSL_net_write_desired 3 .
Only applications which wish to manage their own event loops need to use
these functions; see
.Sy APPLICATION-DRIVEN EVENT LOOPS
for further discussion.
.It
The use of ALPN is mandatory when using QUIC. Attempts to connect
without configuring ALPN will fail.
For information on how to configure ALPN, see
.Xr SSL_set_alpn_protos 3 .
.It
Whether QUIC operates in a client or server mode is determined by the
.Sy SSL_METHOD
used, rather than by calls to
.Xr SSL_set_connect_state 3
or
.Xr SSL_set_accept_state 3 .
It is not necessary to call either of
.Xr SSL_set_connect_state 3
or
.Xr SSL_set_accept_state 3
before connecting, but if either of these are called, the function
called must be congruent with the
.Sy SSL_METHOD
being used.
.It
The
.Xr SSL_set_min_proto_version 3
and
.Xr SSL_set_max_proto_version 3
APIs are not used and the values passed to them are ignored, as OpenSSL
QUIC currently always uses TLS 1.3.
.It
The following libssl functionality is not available when used with QUIC.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It
Async functionality
.It
.Sy SSL_MODE_AUTO_RETRY
.It
Record Padding and Fragmentation
.Pf ( Xr SSL_set_block_padding 3 ,
etc.)
.It
.Xr SSL_stateless 3
support
.It
SRTP functionality
.It
TLSv1.3 Early Data
.It
TLS Next Protocol Negotiation cannot be used and is superseded by ALPN,
which must be used instead.
The use of ALPN is mandatory with QUIC.
.It
Post-Handshake Client Authentication is not available as QUIC prohibits
its use.
.It
QUIC requires the use of TLSv1.3 or later, therefore functionality only
relevant to older TLS versions is not available.
.It
Some cipher suites which are generally available for TLSv1.3 are not
available for QUIC, such as
.Sy TLS_AES_128_CCM_8_SHA256 .
Your application may need to adjust the list of acceptable cipher suites
it passes to libssl.
.It
CCM mode is not currently supported.
.El
.Pp
The following libssl functionality is also not available when used with
QUIC, but calls to the relevant functions are treated as no-ops:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It
Readahead
.Pf ( Xr SSL_set_read_ahead 3 ,
etc.)
.El
.El
.Sh CONSIDERATIONS FOR EXISTING APPLICATIONS
Existing applications seeking to adopt QUIC should apply the following
list to determine what changes they will need to make:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It
A client application wishing to use QUIC must use
.Xr OSSL_QUIC_client_method 3
or
.Xr OSSL_QUIC_client_thread_method 3
as its SSL method.
For more information on the differences between these two methods, see
.Sy THREAD ASSISTED MODE .
.It
A server application wishing to use QUIC must use
.Xr OSSL_QUIC_server_method 3 .
The server can then accept new connections with
.Xr SSL_accept_connection 3 .
.It
Determine how to provide QUIC with network access.
Determine which of the below apply for your application:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It
Your application uses
.Xr BIO_s_socket 3
to construct a BIO which is passed to the SSL object to provide it with
network access.
.Pp
Changes needed: Change your application to use
.Xr BIO_s_datagram 3
instead when using QUIC. The socket must be configured in nonblocking
mode.
You may or may not need to use
.Xr SSL_set1_initial_peer_addr 3
to set the initial peer address; see the
.Sy QUIC-SPECIFIC APIS
section for details.
.It
Your application uses
.Xr BIO_new_ssl_connect 3
to construct a BIO which is passed to the SSL object to provide it with
network access.
.Pp
Changes needed: No changes needed.
Use of QUIC is detected automatically and a datagram socket is created
instead of a normal TCP socket.
.It
Your application uses any other I/O strategy in this list but combines
it with a
.Xr BIO_f_buffer 3 ,
for example using
.Xr BIO_push 3 .
.Pp
Changes needed: Disable the usage of
.Xr BIO_f_buffer 3
when using QUIC. Usage of such a buffer is incompatible with QUIC as
QUIC requires datagram semantics in its interaction with the network.
.It
Your application uses a BIO pair to cause the SSL object to read and
write network traffic to a memory buffer.
Your application manages the transmission and reception of buffered data
itself in a way unknown to libssl.
.Pp
Changes needed: Switch from using a conventional BIO pair to using
.Xr BIO_s_dgram_pair 3
instead, which has the necessary datagram semantics.
You will need to modify your application to transmit and receive using a
UDP socket and to use datagram semantics when interacting with the
.Xr BIO_s_dgram_pair 3
instance.
.It
Your application uses a custom BIO method to provide the SSL object with
network access.
.Pp
Changes needed: The custom BIO must be re-architected to have datagram
semantics.
.Xr BIO_sendmmsg 3
and
.Xr BIO_recvmmsg 3
must be implemented.
These calls must operate in a nonblocking fashion.
Optionally, implement the
.Xr BIO_get_rpoll_descriptor 3
and
.Xr BIO_get_wpoll_descriptor 3
methods if desired.
Implementing these methods is required if blocking semantics at the SSL
API level are desired.
.El
.It
An application must explicitly configure whether it wishes to use the
SSL APIs in blocking mode or not.
Traditionally, an SSL object has automatically operated in blocking or
nonblocking mode based on whether the underlying network BIO operates in
blocking or nonblocking mode.
QUIC requires the use of a nonblocking network BIO, therefore the
blocking mode at the application level can be explicitly configured by
the application using the new
.Xr SSL_set_blocking_mode 3
API. The default mode is blocking.
If an application wishes to use the SSL object APIs at application level
in a nonblocking manner, it must add a call to
.Xr SSL_set_blocking_mode 3
to disable blocking mode.
.It
If your client application does not choose to use thread assisted mode,
it must ensure that it calls an I/O function on the SSL object (for
example,
.Xr SSL_read 3
or
.Xr SSL_write 3 Ns ),
or the new function
.Xr SSL_handle_events 3 ,
regularly.
If the SSL object is used in blocking mode, an ongoing blocking call to
an I/O function satisfies this requirement.
This is required to ensure that timer events required by QUIC are
handled in a timely fashion.
.Pp
Most applications will service the SSL object by calling
.Xr SSL_read 3
or
.Xr SSL_write 3
regularly.
If an application does not do this, it should ensure that
.Xr SSL_handle_events 3
is called regularly.
.Pp
.Xr SSL_get_event_timeout 3
can be used to determine when
.Xr SSL_handle_events 3
must next be called.
.Pp
If the SSL object is being used with an underlying network BIO which is
pollable (such as
.Xr BIO_s_datagram 3 Ns ),
the application can use
.Xr SSL_get_rpoll_descriptor 3 ,
.Xr SSL_get_wpoll_descriptor 3
to obtain resources which can be used to determine when
.Xr SSL_handle_events 3
should be called due to network I/O.
.Pp
Client applications which use thread assisted mode do not need to be
concerned with this requirement, as the QUIC implementation ensures
timeout events are handled in a timely manner.
See
.Sy THREAD ASSISTED MODE
for details.
.It
Ensure that your usage of
.Xr SSL_want 3 ,
.Xr SSL_want_read 3
and
.Xr SSL_want_write 3
reflects the API changes described in
.Sy CHANGES TO EXISTING APIS .
In particular, you should use these APIs to determine the ability of a
QUIC stream to receive or provide application data, not to to determine
if network I/O is required.
.It
Evaluate your application's use of
.Xr SSL_shutdown 3
in light of the changes discussed in
.Sy CHANGES TO EXISTING APIS .
Depending on whether your application wishes to prioritise RFC
conformance or rapid shutdown, consider using the new
.Xr SSL_shutdown_ex 3
API instead.
See
.Sy QUIC-SPECIFIC APIS
for details.
.El
.Sh RECOMMENDED USAGE IN NEW APPLICATIONS
The recommended usage in new applications varies depending on three
independent design decisions:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It
Whether the application will use blocking or nonblocking I/O at the
application level (configured using
.Xr SSL_set_blocking_mode 3 Ns ).
.Pp
If the application does nonblocking I/O at the application level it can
choose to manage its own polling and event loop; see
.Sy APPLICATION-DRIVEN EVENT LOOPS .
.It
Whether the application intends to give the QUIC implementation direct
access to a network socket (e.g.
via
.Xr BIO_s_datagram 3 )
or whether it intends to buffer transmitted and received datagrams via a
.Xr BIO_s_dgram_pair 3
or custom BIO.
.Pp
The former is preferred where possible as it reduces latency to the
network, which enables QUIC to achieve higher performance and more
accurate connection round trip time (RTT) estimation.
.It
Whether thread assisted mode will be used (see
.Sy THREAD ASSISTED MODE Ns ).
.El
.Pp
Simple demos for QUIC usage under these various scenarios can be found
at
.Lk https://github.com/openssl/openssl/tree/master/doc/designs/ddd .
.Pp
Applications which wish to implement QUIC-specific protocols should be
aware of the APIs listed under
.Sy QUIC-SPECIFIC APIS
which provide access to QUIC-specific functionality.
For example,
.Xr SSL_stream_conclude 3
can be used to indicate the end of the sending part of a stream, and
.Xr SSL_shutdown_ex 3
can be used to provide a QUIC application error code when closing a
connection.
.Pp
Regardless of the design decisions chosen above, it is recommended that
new applications avoid use of the default stream mode and use the
multi-stream API by calling
.Xr SSL_set_default_stream_mode 3 ;
see the MODES OF OPERATION section for details.
.Sh QUIC-SPECIFIC APIS
This section details new APIs which are directly or indirectly related
to QUIC. For details on the operation of each API, see the referenced
man pages.
.Pp
The following SSL APIs are new but relevant to both QUIC and DTLS:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xr SSL_get_event_timeout 3
Determines when the QUIC implementation should next be woken up via a
call to
.Xr SSL_handle_events 3
(or another I/O function such as
.Xr SSL_read 3
or
.Xr SSL_write 3 Ns ),
if ever.
.Pp
This can also be used with DTLS and supersedes
.Xr DTLSv1_get_timeout 3
for new usage.
.It Xr SSL_handle_events 3
This is a non-specific I/O operation which makes a best effort attempt
to perform any pending I/O or timeout processing.
It can be used to advance the QUIC state machine by processing incoming
network traffic, generating outgoing network traffic and handling any
expired timeout events.
Most other I/O functions on an SSL object, such as
.Xr SSL_read 3
and
.Xr SSL_write 3 ,
implicitly perform event handling on the SSL object, so calling this
function is only needed if no other I/O function is to be called.
.Pp
This can also be used with DTLS and supersedes
.Xr DTLSv1_handle_timeout 3
for new usage.
.El
.Pp
The following SSL APIs are specific to QUIC:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xr SSL_new_listener 3
Creates a listener SSL object, which differs from an ordinary SSL object
in that it is used to provide an abstraction for the acceptance of
network connections in a protocol-agnostic manner.
.Pp
Currently, listener SSL objects are only supported for QUIC server usage
or client-only usage.
The listener interface may expand to support additional protocols in the
future.
.It Xr SSL_new_listener_from 3
Creates a listener SSL object which is subordinate to a QUIC domain SSL
object
.Em ssl .
See
.Xr SSL_new_domain 3
and
.Xr openssl-quic-concurrency 7
for details on QUIC domain SSL objects.
.It Xr SSL_is_listener 3
Returns 1 if and only if an SSL object is a listener SSL object.
.It Xr SSL_get0_listener 3
Returns an SSL object pointer (potentially to the same object on which
it is called) or NULL.
.It Xr SSL_listen 3
Begin listening after a listener has been created.
It is ordinarily not needed to call this because it will be called
automatically on the first call to
.Xr SSL_accept_connection 3 .
.It Xr SSL_accept_connection 3
Accepts a new incoming connection for a listner SSL object.
A new SSL object representing the accepted connection is created and
returned on success.
If no incoming connection is available and the listener SSL object is
configured in nonblocking mode, NULL is returned.
.It Xr SSL_get_accept_connection_queue_len 3
Returns an informational value listing the number of connections waiting
to be popped from the queue via calls to
.Xr SSL_accept_connection 3 .
.It Xr SSL_new_from_listener 3
Creates a client connection under a given listener SSL object.
For QUIC, it is also possible to use
.Xr SSL_new_from_listener 3
in conjunction with a listener which does accept incoming connections
(i.e., which was not created using
.Sy SSL_LISTENER_FLAG_NO_ACCEPT Ns ),
leading to a UDP network endpoint which has both incoming and outgoing
connections.
.It Xr SSL_new_domain 3
Creates a new QUIC event domain, represented as an SSL object.
This is known as a QUIC domain SSL object.
The concept of a QUIC event domain is discussed in detail in
.Xr openssl-quic-concurrency 7 .
.It Xr SSL_is_domain 3
Returns 1 if an SSL object is a QUIC domain SSL object.
.It Xr SSL_get0_domain 3
.Xr SSL_get0_domain 3
obtains a pointer to the QUIC domain SSL object in an SSL object
hierarchy (if any).
.It Xr SSL_set_blocking_mode 3 Ns , Xr SSL_get_blocking_mode 3
Configures whether blocking semantics are used at the application level.
This determines whether calls to functions such as
.Xr SSL_read 3
and
.Xr SSL_write 3
will block.
.It Xr SSL_get_rpoll_descriptor 3 Ns , Xr SSL_get_wpoll_descriptor 3
These functions facilitate operation in nonblocking mode.
.Pp
When an SSL object is being used with an underlying network read BIO
which supports polling,
.Xr SSL_get_rpoll_descriptor 3
outputs an OS resource which can be used to synchronise on network
readability events which should result in a call to
.Xr SSL_handle_events 3 .
.Xr SSL_get_wpoll_descriptor 3
works in an analogous fashion for the underlying network write BIO.
.Pp
The poll descriptors provided by these functions should be used only
when
.Xr SSL_net_read_desired 3
and
.Xr SSL_net_write_desired 3
return 1, respectively.
.It Xr SSL_net_read_desired 3 Ns , Xr SSL_net_write_desired 3
These functions facilitate operation in nonblocking mode and are used in
conjunction with
.Xr SSL_get_rpoll_descriptor 3
and
.Xr SSL_get_wpoll_descriptor 3
respectively.
They determine whether the respective poll descriptor is currently
relevant for the purposes of polling.
.It Xr SSL_set1_initial_peer_addr 3
This function can be used to set the initial peer address for an
outgoing QUIC connection.
This function must be used in the general case when creating an outgoing
QUIC connection; however, the correct initial peer address can be
autodetected in some cases.
See
.Xr SSL_set1_initial_peer_addr 3
for details.
.It Xr SSL_shutdown_ex 3
This augments
.Xr SSL_shutdown 3
by allowing an application error code to be specified.
It also allows an application to decide how quickly it wants a shutdown
to be performed, potentially by trading off strict RFC compliance.
.It Xr SSL_stream_conclude 3
This allows an application to indicate the normal end of the sending
part of a QUIC stream.
This corresponds to the FIN flag in the QUIC RFC. The receiving part of
a stream remains usable.
.It Xr SSL_stream_reset 3
This allows an application to indicate the non-normal termination of the
sending part of a stream.
This corresponds to the RESET_STREAM frame in the QUIC RFC.
.It Xr SSL_get_stream_write_state 3 No and Xr SSL_get_stream_read_state 3
This allows an application to determine the current stream states for
the sending and receiving parts of a stream respectively.
.It Xr SSL_get_stream_write_error_code 3 No and Xr SSL_get_stream_read_error_code 3
This allows an application to determine the application error code which
was signalled by a peer which has performed a non-normal stream
termination of the respective sending or receiving part of a stream, if
any.
.It Xr SSL_get_conn_close_info 3
This allows an application to determine the error code which was
signalled when the local or remote endpoint terminated the QUIC
connection.
.It Xr SSL_get0_connection 3
Gets the QUIC connection SSL object from a QUIC stream SSL object.
.It Xr SSL_is_connection 3
Returns 1 if an SSL object is not a QUIC stream SSL object.
.It Xr SSL_get_stream_type 3
Provides information on the kind of QUIC stream which is attached to the
SSL object.
.It Xr SSL_get_stream_id 3
Returns the QUIC stream ID which the QUIC protocol has associated with a
QUIC stream.
.It Xr SSL_new_stream 3
Creates a new QUIC stream SSL object representing a new,
locally-initiated QUIC stream.
.It Xr SSL_accept_stream 3
Potentially yields a new QUIC stream SSL object representing a new
remotely-initiated QUIC stream, blocking until one is available if the
connection is configured to do so.
.It Xr SSL_get_accept_stream_queue_len 3
Provides information on the number of pending remotely-initiated
streams.
.It Xr SSL_set_incoming_stream_policy 3
Configures how incoming, remotely-initiated streams are handled.
The incoming stream policy can be used to automatically reject streams
created by the peer, or allow them to be handled using
.Xr SSL_accept_stream 3 .
.It Xr SSL_set_default_stream_mode 3
Used to configure or disable default stream mode; see the MODES OF
OPERATION section for details.
.El
.Pp
The following BIO APIs are not specific to QUIC but have been added to
facilitate QUIC-specific requirements and are closely associated with
its use:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xr BIO_s_dgram_pair 3
This is a new BIO method which is similar to a conventional BIO pair but
provides datagram semantics.
.It Xr BIO_get_rpoll_descriptor 3 Ns , Xr BIO_get_wpoll_descriptor 3
This is a new BIO API which allows a BIO to expose a poll descriptor.
This API is used to implement the corresponding SSL APIs
.Xr SSL_get_rpoll_descriptor 3
and
.Xr SSL_get_wpoll_descriptor 3 .
.It Xr BIO_sendmmsg 3 Ns , Xr BIO_recvmmsg 3
This is a new BIO API which can be implemented by BIOs which implement
datagram semantics.
It is implemented by
.Xr BIO_s_datagram 3
and
.Xr BIO_s_dgram_pair 3 .
It is used by the QUIC implementation to send and receive UDP datagrams.
.It Xr BIO_dgram_set_no_trunc 3 Ns , Xr BIO_dgram_get_no_trunc 3
By default,
.Xr BIO_s_dgram_pair 3
has semantics comparable to those of Berkeley sockets being used with
datagram semantics.
This allows an alternative mode to be enabled in which datagrams will
not be silently truncated if they are too large.
.It Xr BIO_dgram_set_caps 3 Ns , Xr BIO_dgram_get_caps 3
These functions are used to allow the user of one end of a
.Xr BIO_s_dgram_pair 3
to indicate its capabilities to the other end of a
.Xr BIO_s_dgram_pair 3 .
In particular, this allows an application to inform the QUIC
implementation of whether it is prepared to handle local and/or peer
addresses in transmitted datagrams and to provide the applicable
information in received datagrams.
.It Xr BIO_dgram_get_local_addr_cap 3 Ns , Xr BIO_dgram_set_local_addr_enable 3 No , Xr BIO_dgram_get_local_addr_enable 3
Local addressing support refers to the ability of a BIO with datagram
semantics to allow a source address to be specified on transmission and
to report the destination address on reception.
These functions can be used to determine if a BIO can support local
addressing and to enable local addressing support if it can.
.It Xr BIO_err_is_non_fatal 3
This is used to determine if an error while calling
.Xr BIO_sendmmsg 3
or
.Xr BIO_recvmmsg 3
is ephemeral in nature, such as "would block" errors.
.El
.Sh THREAD ASSISTED MODE
The optional thread assisted mode for clients can be used with
.Xr OSSL_QUIC_client_thread_method 3 .
In this mode, a background thread is created automatically.
The OpenSSL QUIC implementation then takes responsibility for ensuring
that timeout events are handled on a timely basis even if no SSL I/O
function such as
.Xr SSL_read 3
or
.Xr SSL_write 3
is called by the application for a long time.
.Pp
All necessary locking is handled automatically internally, but the
thread safety guarantees for the public SSL API are unchanged.
Therefore, an application must still do its own locking if it wishes to
make concurrent use of the public SSL APIs.
.Pp
Because this method relies on threads, it is not available on platforms
where threading support is not available or not supported by OpenSSL.
However, it does provide the simplest mode of usage for an application.
.Pp
The implementation may or may not use a common thread or thread pool to
service multiple SSL objects in the same
.Sy SSL_CTX .
.Sh APPLICATION-DRIVEN EVENT LOOPS
OpenSSL's QUIC implementation is designed to facilitate applications
which wish to use the SSL APIs in a blocking fashion, but is also
designed to facilitate applications which wish to use the SSL APIs in a
nonblocking fashion and manage their own event loops and polling
directly.
This is useful when it is desirable to host OpenSSL's QUIC
implementation on top of an application's existing nonblocking I/O
infrastructure.
.Pp
This is supported via the concept of poll descriptors; see
.Xr BIO_get_rpoll_descriptor 3
for details.
Broadly, a
.Sy BIO_POLL_DESCRIPTOR
is a structure which expresses some kind of OS resource which can be
used to synchronise on I/O events.
The QUIC implementation provides a
.Sy BIO_POLL_DESCRIPTOR
based on the poll descriptor provided by the underlying network BIO.
This is typically an OS socket handle, though custom BIOs could choose
to implement their own custom poll descriptor format.
.Pp
Broadly, an application which wishes to manage its own event loop should
interact with the SSL object as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It
It should provide read and write BIOs with nonblocking datagram
semantics to the SSL object using
.Xr SSL_set0_rbio 3
and
.Xr SSL_set0_wbio 3 .
This could be a BIO abstracting a network socket such as
.Xr BIO_s_datagram 3 ,
or a BIO abstracting some kind of memory buffer such as
.Xr BIO_s_dgram_pair 3 .
Use of a custom BIO is also possible.
.It
It should configure the SSL object into nonblocking mode by calling
.Xr SSL_set_blocking_mode 3 .
.It
It should configure the SSL object as desired, set an initial peer as
needed using
.Xr SSL_set1_initial_peer_addr 3 ,
and trigger the connection process by calling
.Xr SSL_connect 3 .
.It
If the network read and write BIOs provided were pollable (for example,
a
.Xr BIO_s_datagram 3 ,
or a custom BIO which implements
.Xr BIO_get_rpoll_descriptor 3
and
.Xr BIO_get_wpoll_descriptor 3 Ns ),
it should perform the following steps repeatedly:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It
The application should call
.Xr SSL_get_rpoll_descriptor 3
and
.Xr SSL_get_wpoll_descriptor 3
to identify OS resources which can be used for synchronisation.
.It
It should call
.Xr SSL_net_read_desired 3
and
.Xr SSL_net_write_desired 3
to determine whether the QUIC implementation is currently interested in
readability and writability events on the underlying network BIO which
was provided, and call
.Xr SSL_get_event_timeout 3
to determine if any timeout event will become applicable in the future.
.It
It should wait until one of the following events occurs:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It
The poll descriptor returned by
.Xr SSL_get_rpoll_descriptor 3
becomes readable (if
.Xr SSL_net_read_desired 3
returned 1);
.It
The poll descriptor returned by
.Xr SSL_get_wpoll_descriptor 3
becomes writable (if
.Xr SSL_net_write_desired 3
returned 1);
.It
The timeout returned by
.Xr SSL_get_event_timeout 3
(if any) expires.
.El
.Pp
Once any of these events occurs,
.Xr SSL_handle_events 3
should be called.
.El
.It
If the network read and write BIOs provided were not pollable (for
example, in the case of
.Xr BIO_s_dgram_pair 3 Ns ),
the application is responsible for managing and synchronising network
I/O. It should call
.Xr SSL_handle_events 3
after it writes data to a
.Xr BIO_s_dgram_pair 3
or otherwise takes action so that the QUIC implementation can read new
datagrams via a call to
.Xr BIO_recvmmsg 3
on the underlying network BIO. The QUIC implementation may output
datagrams via a call to
.Xr BIO_sendmmsg 3
and the application is responsible for ensuring these are transmitted.
.Pp
The application must call
.Xr SSL_get_event_timeout 3
after every call to
.Xr SSL_handle_events 3
(or another I/O function on the SSL object), and ensure that a call to
.Xr SSL_handle_events 3
is performed after the specified timeout (if any).
.El
